      "writtenOn": "2026-10-05T02:44:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Each threshold is evaluated independently. This is the existing behavior, not something this patch changes. When the reading exceeds Critical, both Critical and Warning get asserted. Here is the journal from the current master, without this patch (both thresholds with `Log` enabled):\n\n```\n18:36:21 ASSERT: Health Metric CPU crossed ...Type.Critical upper threshold\n18:36:21 ASSERT: Health Metric CPU crossed ...Type.Warning upper threshold\n18:37:08 DEASSERT: Health Metric CPU is below ...Type.Critical upper threshold\n18:37:42 DEASSERT: Health Metric CPU is below ...Type.Warning upper threshold\n18:38:47 ASSERT: Health Metric CPU crossed ...Type.Warning upper threshold\n18:39:25 ASSERT: Health Metric CPU crossed ...Type.Critical upper threshold\n18:39:58 DEASSERT: Health Metric CPU is below ...Type.Critical upper threshold\n18:40:34 DEASSERT: Health Metric CPU is below ...Type.Warning upper threshold\n```\n\nThis patch only adds an event log to each of these assertions and keeps the same flow. When the reading drops below Critical but stays within the Warning range (e.g. 18:37:08):\n- The Critical event log is resolved.\n- The Warning event log stays unresolved, since Warning is still asserted.\n\nSo we don\u0027t log a new warning message: the outstanding Warning entry already shows the metric is still in the warning range. Once no logged threshold is outstanding (e.g. 18:37:42), a `TriggerNumericReadingNormal` event is logged.\n\n`TestCriticalAndWarningLogged` in test_health_metric_event.cpp covers this sequence.",
      "parentUuid": "dd87fdfb_2a8ae475",
      "revId": "5c318a5d2be53d56c5c60eec395caf93db63c5b1",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
